    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "1936040217578029446",
      "url": "https://x.com/thinkinboutwonu/status/1936040217578029446",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/thinkinboutwonu/status/1936040217578029446",
      "text": "random life reflection no one asked for but i will share anyway: tbh pre pandemic, my career choices and growth was limited because of where I lived (qc) and the traffic/travel time/energy/cost to makati or bgc. I literally only applied and worked in qc, the farthest was ortigas for a bit, and only because it was my dream job. \n\ni feel that i could’ve grown faster and further if I took jobs at big companies in makati/bgc earlier. the only reason why i accepted a bgc-based office was because it was 2021, wfh. maaga kami nag RTO in 2022 but traffic was manageable. kaya ko bgc to home in 45 mins dati basta 8:30pm ako umalis. where i work now is hybrid 3 days RTO but to be honest these past few months ive been feeling the difficulty talaga. it’s like okay i’ve made it to qc pero commonwealth is the other half of the battle.\n\nsoooooobrang scary to drive alone at night lately. and for some reason i’ve been feeling much more anxious driving in commonwealth at night these past few weeks. di ko alam, the vibes are just off.\n\nit’s so exhausting, I spend so much time on the road no matter how long ako magpalipas. last tuesday i left bgc at 9, got home at 11. it’s crazy. i love my home, it was the first space that I really had full control over from the layout, colors, everything. i’m a qc/up girl, i also need my trees and mountains.\n\ni acknowledge how privileged i am to have my own car, a house, a job. but im so exhausted too and every day that goes by I feel that I really can’t live like this. and when i think about it there’s nothing for me in qc anymore now that i’m at this stage of my career.\n\nso ayun. i might move, but with that comes a whole new set of things to think about. and in the past what i’d say was: i refuse to uproot my life for a job. but lately ive been feeling that my job has been uprooting my life. \n\nat the end of the day: if we had an efficient public transport system, if our streets were walkable, if simply existing as a woman alone at night was safe, so many of us wouldn’t have to make these difficult choices and uproot our lives (or let our lives be uprooted) just to survive.",
      "source": "Twitter for iPhone",
